      agriculture: { title: 'Agriculture stores sunlight as surplus', body: 'Farming, beginning around 10,000 BCE in several regions, was not simply "better food." It was a new energy system. Grain stores calories for months, supports taxation, armies, cities, writing, and hierarchy. It also made diets narrower and disease easier to spread, which is a useful reminder: technologies solve one problem while creating new ones.' },
      industry: { title: 'Industry replaces muscle with fuel', body: 'The steam engine made coal into mechanical work anywhere you could move fuel and water. Britain produced roughly 10 million tons of coal in 1800 and more than 200 million by 1900. Factories reorganised time itself: instead of work following seasons and daylight, people followed clocks, shifts, wages, and machines.' },
      electrical: { title: 'Electricity separates power from place', body: 'Electrification let energy travel through wires and be converted into light, heat, motion, sound, or computation. The 1882 Pearl Street Station in New York served only a small district; within decades, grids became civilisation-scale infrastructure. The underrated miracle is standardisation: outlets, voltage, motors, transformers, and billing systems all had to agree.' },
      digital: { title: 'Digital technology makes information programmable', body: 'Analog systems represent information continuously; digital systems represent it with discrete states. That makes copying, error correction, compression, encryption, and general-purpose computation easier. A smartphone is not one tool but a universal machine plus sensors, radio, storage, battery chemistry, cameras, maps, payments, and a software marketplace.' },
