    var semiData = {
      fab:        { title: 'Chip fabrication — how a chip is made', body: 'Chip fabrication begins with a silicon wafer (ultrapure silicon, 300mm diameter). Using photolithography, circuits are "printed" onto the wafer by shining extreme ultraviolet (EUV) light through a mask. Up to 100+ layers are deposited and etched. A single 300mm wafer might yield 700 chips. The entire process requires cleanrooms where particle contamination is measured in parts per trillion. TSMC runs over 50 steps of EUV lithography per chip, each one potentially the source of a defect that ruins the entire chip.', meta: 'Only ASML (Netherlands) makes EUV machines, at ~$380M each' },
      tsmc:       { title: 'TSMC — the most important company most people have never heard of', body: 'Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (TSMC), founded in 1987 by Morris Chang, manufactures chips designed by Apple, NVIDIA, AMD, Qualcomm, and most of the world\'s electronics industry. TSMC has ~57% share of global semiconductor fabrication revenue and over 90% of the most advanced node capacity. If Taiwan\'s chip production were disrupted — by earthquake, conflict, or blockade — the global electronics industry would halt within months.', meta: 'Taiwan produces ~65% of global semiconductor value' },
      geopolitics:{ title: 'Chip wars — the geopolitics of semiconductor supply', body: 'The US CHIPS Act (2022, $52 billion) and the EU Chips Act (2023, €43 billion) both attempt to onshore advanced semiconductor manufacturing. US export controls (2022-2023) blocked NVIDIA from selling its most powerful AI chips to China and prevented ASML from supplying EUV machines. China\'s national semiconductor programme exceeds $150 billion in government investment. Neither China nor Europe can yet produce EUV machines — the supply chain spans 5,000+ suppliers across 40 countries, accumulated over 30 years.', meta: 'ASML is a monopoly — there are zero alternative EUV suppliers' },
      nodes:      { title: 'What "nm nodes" actually mean today', body: 'When Intel released its 22nm process in 2012, the nm figure roughly corresponded to a transistor gate length. Since ~14nm, this relationship broke down. "3nm" from TSMC (2022) does not mean any dimension is 3 nanometres — it\'s a marketing label for transistor density and power efficiency. Apple\'s A17 Pro (TSMC 3nm) has ~133 million transistors per mm². ENIAC (1945) had 18,000 vacuum tubes in 680 square feet. The "3nm" chip is roughly 200 billion times denser per unit area.', meta: 'Apple A17 Pro: 19 billion transistors in 66.3mm²' },
      power:      { title: 'Energy and the AI compute crisis', body: 'Training GPT-4 is estimated to have consumed over 50 GWh of electricity — enough to power 4,600 average US homes for a year. A single ChatGPT query uses roughly 10x the electricity of a Google search. Data centres consumed ~200 TWh globally in 2022; AI workloads are projected to push this above 1,000 TWh by 2030. Major tech companies have responded by signing long-term nuclear power agreements: Microsoft contracted output from a restarted Three Mile Island unit; Google signed deals with Kairos Power for small modular reactors.', meta: 'Microsoft, Google, Amazon all signed nuclear power deals in 2024' }
    };
